1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,896
From Borneo to Singapore:

2
00:00:01,976 --> 00:00:05,850
The Sabah and Sarawak
People in Singapore's Story

3
00:00:05,850 --> 00:00:10,546
Elizabeth Choy, the wartime heroine
who survived 200 days in the

4
00:00:10,626 --> 00:00:15,625
hands of the Kempeitai, was born
in Kudat, in British North Borneo.

5
00:00:15,625 --> 00:00:18,249
Hugh Zehnder, the lawyer after whom

6
00:00:18,329 --> 00:00:21,650
Zehnder Road is named,
was born in Sarawak.

7
00:00:21,650 --> 00:00:26,500
So was Wee Kheng Chiang, who
founded the bank that became UOB.

8
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:28,625
They were not exceptions.

9
00:00:28,625 --> 00:00:32,431
For more than a century and a
half, people, money and ships

10
00:00:32,511 --> 00:00:36,318
have moved between Singapore
and the two Borneo territories

11
00:00:36,398 --> 00:00:39,941
that are now the Malaysian
states of Sabah and Sarawak,

12
00:00:40,021 --> 00:00:44,500
and some of the people who came
from there left a lasting mark here.

13
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:48,425
The story begins with a
voyage from Singapore.

14
00:00:48,425 --> 00:00:51,657
In 1839 a young Englishman, James Brooke,

15
00:00:51,737 --> 00:00:56,825
sailed from Singapore to Sarawak
in his schooner, the Royalist.

16
00:00:56,825 --> 00:01:00,405
By 1841 he had become
the Rajah of Sarawak,

17
00:01:00,485 --> 00:01:04,825
and for the next hundred
years his family ruled it.

18
00:01:04,825 --> 00:01:08,417
Kuching was a small river
port, and most of Sarawak's

19
00:01:08,497 --> 00:01:12,100
trade with the outside world
went through Singapore.

20
00:01:12,100 --> 00:01:15,270
Its sago, pepper and
gambier were shipped there,

21
00:01:15,350 --> 00:01:19,075
and the goods its people
bought came back the same way.

22
00:01:19,075 --> 00:01:22,047
Some of the first people
to make the crossing

23
00:01:22,127 --> 00:01:25,450
went from Singapore to
Borneo, not the other way.

24
00:01:25,450 --> 00:01:29,925
Ong Ewe Hai was born in
Singapore around 1830.

25
00:01:29,925 --> 00:01:33,738
His father died when he was
about seven, and as the eldest

26
00:01:33,818 --> 00:01:37,375
child he had little schooling
and went to work young.

27
00:01:37,375 --> 00:01:42,795
In 1846, aged 16, he went to Sarawak
with his mentor, Lim Eng Moh,

28
00:01:42,875 --> 00:01:48,375
to trade with the local people,
using credit from Singapore firms.

29
00:01:48,375 --> 00:01:52,465
Within about ten years he
had a firm in Singapore and

30
00:01:52,545 --> 00:01:56,400
another in Kuching, which
he merged in 1872 as Ewe

31
00:01:56,480 --> 00:02:00,650
Hai and Company, trading in
sago, gambier and pepper.

32
00:02:00,650 --> 00:02:03,258
Brooke appointed him Kapitan Cina,

33
00:02:03,338 --> 00:02:06,500
the head of Kuching's Chinese community.

34
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:10,123
He built a house in Singapore,
called Bonnie Grass,

35
00:02:10,203 --> 00:02:13,173
where he meant to retire, but it was still

36
00:02:13,253 --> 00:02:16,375
unfinished when he died
in Kuching in 1888.

37
00:02:16,375 --> 00:02:19,625
British capital followed the same route.

38
00:02:19,625 --> 00:02:24,907
The Borneo Company was formed in
London in 1856 to trade in Sarawak,

39
00:02:24,987 --> 00:02:29,638
and on the 31st of July 1857
it opened its Singapore branch,

40
00:02:29,718 --> 00:02:33,108
taking over the business
of its agent there,

41
00:02:33,188 --> 00:02:37,525
MacEwen and Company, with
its wharves at Telok Blangah.

42
00:02:37,525 --> 00:02:41,428
In its first years most of
its profit came from antimony,

43
00:02:41,508 --> 00:02:46,050
a metal mined in Sarawak and
shipped to Europe through Singapore.

44
00:02:46,050 --> 00:02:48,884
It later mined gold at Bau, near Kuching,

45
00:02:48,964 --> 00:02:53,300
and grew into a trading house
with offices across the region.

46
00:02:53,300 --> 00:02:57,791
Further north, the British
North Borneo Company received a

47
00:02:57,871 --> 00:03:02,600
royal charter in 1881 to govern
North Borneo, today's Sabah.

48
00:03:02,600 --> 00:03:06,389
Its officials, too, dealt
constantly with Singapore,

49
00:03:06,469 --> 00:03:08,775
the nearest large British port.

50
00:03:08,775 --> 00:03:12,681
Labuan, a small island off
the coast, was even governed

51
00:03:12,761 --> 00:03:16,667
from Singapore for a time:
from the 1st of January 1907

52
00:03:16,747 --> 00:03:20,653
it was part of the Settlement
of Singapore, and in 1912

53
00:03:20,733 --> 00:03:25,219
it became a separate settlement
within the Straits Settlements,

54
00:03:25,299 --> 00:03:28,850
until it was transferred
to North Borneo in 1946.

55
00:03:28,850 --> 00:03:32,575
The traffic in people
soon ran the other way.

56
00:03:32,575 --> 00:03:36,055
Ong Ewe Hai's son, Ong
Tiang Swee, was born in

57
00:03:36,135 --> 00:03:40,157
Kuching in 1864, went to
school there, and then spent

58
00:03:40,237 --> 00:03:43,875
two years in Singapore
to finish his education.

59
00:03:43,875 --> 00:03:48,318
In 1882 he joined his father's
firm, and in 1887 he became

60
00:03:48,398 --> 00:03:52,841
a director of the Sarawak and
Singapore Steamship Company,

61
00:03:52,921 --> 00:03:57,600
whose ships were Sarawak's main
link with the outside world.

62
00:03:57,600 --> 00:04:02,302
He succeeded his father as
Kapitan Cina in 1889 and led

63
00:04:02,382 --> 00:04:07,425
Kuching's Chinese community
for most of the next 60 years.

64
00:04:07,425 --> 00:04:11,348
Hugh Ransome Stanley
Zehnder was born in Sarawak

65
00:04:11,428 --> 00:04:16,935
on the 27th of August 1879, the
son of a Swiss Anglican missionary,

66
00:04:17,015 --> 00:04:19,350
and was of Eurasian descent.

67
00:04:19,350 --> 00:04:23,203
He came to Singapore in
August 1909 and was called

68
00:04:23,283 --> 00:04:26,979
to the bar on the 23rd
of May 1910, and with his

69
00:04:27,059 --> 00:04:31,150
brother he founded the law
firm of Zehnder Brothers.

70
00:04:31,150 --> 00:04:34,786
He was a founding member of the Eurasian

71
00:04:34,866 --> 00:04:39,325
Association in 1919 and
its president from 1936.

72
00:04:39,325 --> 00:04:43,243
In 1918 he helped form the
Eurasian company of the

73
00:04:43,323 --> 00:04:47,402
Singapore Volunteer Corps,
which he later commanded,

74
00:04:47,482 --> 00:04:51,800
and he was made an OBE for
his volunteer work in 1930.

75
00:04:51,800 --> 00:04:55,892
From 1936 to 1939 he was
the Eurasian member of

76
00:04:55,972 --> 00:05:00,500
the Legislative Council of
the Straits Settlements.

77
00:05:00,500 --> 00:05:05,471
After the war he sat on the
Singapore Advisory Council and served

78
00:05:05,551 --> 00:05:10,525
as a civil district judge, before
he retired to England in 1948.

79
00:05:10,525 --> 00:05:14,153
He had owned land off Pasir
Panjang Road and lived

80
00:05:14,233 --> 00:05:17,564
on Pasir Panjang Hill,
and Zehnder Road there,

81
00:05:17,644 --> 00:05:21,500
which runs beside Haw Par
Villa, is named after him.

82
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:24,575
He died in 1963.

83
00:05:24,575 --> 00:05:27,425
Money moved both ways too.

84
00:05:27,425 --> 00:05:31,636
Chee Swee Cheng was born
in Malacca in 1866 and

85
00:05:31,716 --> 00:05:35,825
came to Singapore at 16
to work as a cashier.

86
00:05:35,825 --> 00:05:39,570
In 1900 he became a partner
in the General Spirit

87
00:05:39,650 --> 00:05:43,551
and Opium Farm in North
Borneo, the licence to sell

88
00:05:43,631 --> 00:05:47,300
alcohol and opium there,
and later its manager.

89
00:05:47,300 --> 00:05:51,374
The North Borneo government
granted him 5,000 acres of land,

90
00:05:51,454 --> 00:05:54,352
on which he planted
tapioca and then rubber

91
00:05:54,432 --> 00:05:57,883
and set up a sawmill, and
he also built brickfields

92
00:05:57,963 --> 00:06:01,425
and shophouses and founded
two scholarships there.

93
00:06:01,425 --> 00:06:05,780
Back in Singapore, he
co-founded Ho Hong Bank in 1917,

94
00:06:05,860 --> 00:06:11,282
and when it merged with two other
Chinese banks in 1932 to form the

95
00:06:11,362 --> 00:06:17,275
Oversea-Chinese Banking Corporation,
OCBC, he became its first chairman.

96
00:06:17,275 --> 00:06:22,425
He died in Singapore on
the 22nd of August 1938.

97
00:06:22,425 --> 00:06:26,225
The two family stories
met in Wee Kheng Chiang.

98
00:06:26,225 --> 00:06:29,852
He was born in Kuching
on the 22nd of July 1890,

99
00:06:29,932 --> 00:06:34,099
married Ong Tiang Swee's
eldest daughter, Ong Siew Eng,

100
00:06:34,179 --> 00:06:39,504
and managed a bank owned by his
father-in-law before founding his own,

101
00:06:39,584 --> 00:06:42,750
the Bian Chiang Bank, in Kuching in 1924.

102
00:06:42,750 --> 00:06:46,658
In 1935 he led a group
of seven businessmen in

103
00:06:46,738 --> 00:06:50,725
founding the United
Chinese Bank in Singapore.

104
00:06:50,725 --> 00:06:56,971
It was incorporated on the 6th of August
and opened on the 1st of October 1935,

105
00:06:57,051 --> 00:07:00,975
with Wee as its chairman
and largest shareholder.

106
00:07:00,975 --> 00:07:05,642
Wee kept his home in Kuching,
where he died in 1978,

107
00:07:05,722 --> 00:07:08,562
but the bank stayed in Singapore

108
00:07:08,642 --> 00:07:12,750
and was renamed United
Overseas Bank in 1965.

109
00:07:12,750 --> 00:07:18,175
His son, Wee Cho Yaw, led it for
decades, as the UOB post tells.

110
00:07:18,175 --> 00:07:21,295
Elizabeth Choy was born Yong Su-Moi

111
00:07:21,375 --> 00:07:24,850
in Kudat on the 29th of November 1910.

112
00:07:24,850 --> 00:07:28,637
She went to school in Kudat
and at Saint Monica's,

113
00:07:28,717 --> 00:07:34,050
an Anglican boarding school in
Sandakan, and in December 1929 she came

114
00:07:34,130 --> 00:07:38,925
to Singapore to study at the
Convent of the Holy Infant Jesus.

115
00:07:38,925 --> 00:07:42,622
She stayed on to teach,
and in 1941 she married

116
00:07:42,702 --> 00:07:46,800
Choy Khun Heng, a bookkeeper
at the Borneo Company.

117
00:07:46,800 --> 00:07:50,734
During the Japanese Occupation
the couple ran a canteen

118
00:07:50,814 --> 00:07:54,456
at the Mental Hospital and
used it to smuggle food,

119
00:07:54,536 --> 00:07:58,550
medicine, money and letters
to the internees in Changi.

120
00:07:58,550 --> 00:08:01,589
In 1943, after the Double Tenth arrests,

121
00:08:01,669 --> 00:08:06,737
both were detained by the Kempeitai,
and she was held and tortured

122
00:08:06,817 --> 00:08:10,950
for about 200 days without
giving them a single name.

123
00:08:10,950 --> 00:08:14,821
After the war she was made
an OBE, and the Rajah of

124
00:08:14,901 --> 00:08:18,775
Sarawak gave her the Order
of the Star of Sarawak.

125
00:08:18,775 --> 00:08:24,775
From 1951 to 1955 she was the only woman
on Singapore's Legislative Council,

126
00:08:24,855 --> 00:08:26,775
and she went on to be the

127
00:08:26,855 --> 00:08:31,175
first principal of the
Singapore School for the Blind.

128
00:08:31,175 --> 00:08:35,075
Her full story is in
the Elizabeth Choy post.

129
00:08:35,075 --> 00:08:38,325
After the war, Singapore
was the centre of the

130
00:08:38,405 --> 00:08:41,583
Malay film industry,
with studios run by Shaw

131
00:08:41,663 --> 00:08:45,500
Brothers' Malay Film
Productions and by Cathay Keris.

132
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:49,425
One of its best-known
actresses came from Kuching.

133
00:08:49,425 --> 00:08:54,803
Ramlah binti Mohamad, known as Siput
Sarawak, was born there in 1921,

134
00:08:54,883 --> 00:08:59,153
and by the age of about 11
she was acting in bangsawan,

135
00:08:59,233 --> 00:09:03,900
Malay opera, with her father,
the performer Dollah Sarawak.

136
00:09:03,900 --> 00:09:07,824
After the war she sang
in cabarets in Singapore,

137
00:09:07,904 --> 00:09:13,075
and in 1947 she made her first
film, Singapura di Waktu Malam.

138
00:09:13,075 --> 00:09:17,865
She went on to act in about 50
films for Malay Film Productions,

139
00:09:17,945 --> 00:09:20,299
Cathay Keris and Merdeka Studio,

140
00:09:20,379 --> 00:09:24,712
often as the villain, and in
1952 the Straits Times Annual

141
00:09:24,792 --> 00:09:28,825
called her one of Malaya's
best-known film actresses.

142
00:09:28,825 --> 00:09:32,260
Her daughters, the actress
Murni Sarawak and the

143
00:09:32,340 --> 00:09:36,075
singer Anita Sarawak, both
became entertainers too.

144
00:09:36,075 --> 00:09:40,463
Siput Sarawak died in
Singapore on the 3rd of June

145
00:09:40,543 --> 00:09:45,100
1999 and is buried at Pusara
Abadi in Lim Chu Kang.

146
00:09:45,100 --> 00:09:46,803
For two years Singapore,

147
00:09:46,883 --> 00:09:50,450
Sabah and Sarawak were
part of the same country.

148
00:09:50,450 --> 00:09:54,656
In 1962 a commission led by
Lord Cobbold went to North

149
00:09:54,736 --> 00:09:59,497
Borneo and Sarawak to find out
whether their people supported

150
00:09:59,577 --> 00:10:03,625
joining a new federation
with Malaya and Singapore.

151
00:10:03,625 --> 00:10:07,930
Its report, signed in June and
published on the 1st of August 1962,

152
00:10:08,010 --> 00:10:12,184
found that about a third of the
people in each territory strongly

153
00:10:12,264 --> 00:10:16,372
favoured the plan and another
third favoured it with safeguards,

154
00:10:16,452 --> 00:10:20,495
while the remaining third were
divided between those who wanted

155
00:10:20,575 --> 00:10:25,025
independence first and those who
preferred British rule to continue.

156
00:10:25,025 --> 00:10:30,756
The Malaysia Agreement was signed
in London on the 9th of July 1963,

157
00:10:30,836 --> 00:10:35,199
and on the 16th of September
1963 Malaya, Singapore,

158
00:10:35,279 --> 00:10:40,150
North Borneo, renamed Sabah,
and Sarawak became Malaysia.

159
00:10:40,150 --> 00:10:42,475
The union was short.

160
00:10:42,475 --> 00:10:46,556
On the 9th of August 1965
Singapore separated from

161
00:10:46,636 --> 00:10:50,134
Malaysia and became an
independent country,

162
00:10:50,214 --> 00:10:54,375
while Sabah and Sarawak
remained Malaysian states.

163
00:10:54,375 --> 00:10:57,738
A border now ran between
them and Singapore,

164
00:10:57,818 --> 00:10:59,775
but the old ties went on.

165
00:10:59,775 --> 00:11:04,132
Sabahans and Sarawakians still come
to Singapore to study and work,

166
00:11:04,212 --> 00:11:08,371
and they are among the many
Malaysians in Singapore's workforce,

167
00:11:08,451 --> 00:11:12,425
although no official figures
count them separately by state.

168
00:11:12,425 --> 00:11:14,925
Their food has come with them.

169
00:11:14,925 --> 00:11:19,780
Kolo mee, Kuching's springy egg
noodles tossed in lard and shallot oil,

170
00:11:19,860 --> 00:11:23,395
and Sarawak laksa, a spiced
coconut soup with prawns

171
00:11:23,475 --> 00:11:27,496
and omelette strips, are sold
at a handful of hawker stalls

172
00:11:27,576 --> 00:11:32,025
across the island, several of
them run by families from Sarawak.

173
00:11:32,025 --> 00:11:35,756
One stall owner from Sarikei
told a food writer that

174
00:11:35,836 --> 00:11:39,859
she had opened in Bedok because
the Sarawakian community

175
00:11:39,939 --> 00:11:43,750
in Singapore was small and
missed the taste of home.

176
00:11:43,750 --> 00:11:47,200
Singaporeans travel the other way, too.

177
00:11:47,200 --> 00:11:52,425
Mount Kinabalu, in Sabah, is a popular
climb for visitors from Singapore,

178
00:11:52,505 --> 00:11:55,775
and some schools take
groups of pupils there.

179
00:11:55,775 --> 00:12:00,845
On the morning of the 5th of June
2015 an earthquake of magnitude

180
00:12:00,925 --> 00:12:05,600
6.0 struck the mountain and
killed 18 people on its slopes.

181
00:12:05,600 --> 00:12:08,723
Ten of them were
Singaporeans: seven pupils and

182
00:12:08,803 --> 00:12:11,994
two teachers from Tanjong
Katong Primary School,

183
00:12:12,074 --> 00:12:15,550
and an adventure guide
travelling with their group.

184
00:12:15,550 --> 00:12:18,906
Four were Sabahan mountain
guides, Robbi Sapinggi,

185
00:12:18,986 --> 00:12:22,480
Valerian Joannes, Ricky
Masirin and Joseph Solungin,

186
00:12:22,560 --> 00:12:26,122
who died while protecting
the climbers in their care,

187
00:12:26,202 --> 00:12:30,325
and other local guides were the
first rescuers on the scene.

188
00:12:30,325 --> 00:12:35,028
Singapore held a day of national
remembrance on the 8th of June,

189
00:12:35,108 --> 00:12:39,287
with flags at half-mast, and
in 2016 a plaque bearing the

190
00:12:39,367 --> 00:12:44,225
names of all 18 victims was put
up near the foot of the mountain.

191
00:12:44,225 --> 00:12:47,600
Other traces are easy to walk past.

192
00:12:47,600 --> 00:12:51,751
UOB's headquarters at Raffles
Place stands on the site of the

193
00:12:51,831 --> 00:12:56,200
building where Wee Kheng Chiang's
bank rented its first office.

194
00:12:56,200 --> 00:13:00,429
Zehnder Road still carries the
name of a Sarawak-born lawyer,

195
00:13:00,509 --> 00:13:05,022
and the Heeren, at the corner of
Orchard Road and Cairnhill Road,

196
00:13:05,102 --> 00:13:07,847
keeps the name of the building Chee Swee

197
00:13:07,927 --> 00:13:12,025
Cheng put up there in 1931,
which was pulled down in 1990.

198
00:13:12,025 --> 00:13:16,270
Where it fits in the bigger story:
Singapore's history is usually

199
00:13:16,350 --> 00:13:19,663
told through the people
who came from China, India,

200
00:13:19,743 --> 00:13:24,800
the Malay world and Europe, and the
Borneo territories rarely get a mention.

201
00:13:24,800 --> 00:13:28,430
Yet for more than 150 years
Sarawak and North Borneo were

202
00:13:28,510 --> 00:13:31,685
part of Singapore's
hinterland: their trade passed

203
00:13:31,765 --> 00:13:35,134
through its port, their
children came to its schools,

204
00:13:35,214 --> 00:13:39,250
and their money helped build two
of its three big local banks.

205
00:13:39,250 --> 00:13:43,277
Some of the people who made that
crossing, like Elizabeth Choy,

206
00:13:43,357 --> 00:13:48,050
Hugh Zehnder and Wee Kheng Chiang,
became part of Singapore's own story.

207
00:13:48,050 --> 00:13:52,489
Many others, the traders, students
and workers who came and went,

208
00:13:52,569 --> 00:13:55,975
left no record at all,
and they still come today.
